Instead of deleting all the rows at once, which could increase the log file size, you should delete the data in small batches. This helps to manage the transaction log growth. Please perform this operation during periods of lower activity to avoid slowing down your database's performance during peak hours.

Here's an example of how you can do a batch delete in SQL Server:

```sql
WHILE (1 = 1)
BEGIN
    BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    DELETE TOP (10000) FROM your_table
    WHERE your_condition;
    IF @@ROWCOUNT < 10000 BREAK;
    COMMIT TRANSACTION;
    CHECKPOINT; -- for simple recovery model
    WAITFOR DELAY '00:00:05';
END;
```

This script deletes 10,000 rows at a time, then pauses for 5 seconds before starting the new batch.

