Begin by noticing that $81 = 9^2$. We can then apply a difference of squares factorization and further factorize the resulting expressions:
\begin{align*}
x^6 - 81 &= (x^3 + 9)(x^3 - 9) \\
&= (x^3 + 9)(x - 3)(x^2 + 3x + 9) \\
&= \boxed{(x^3 + 9)(x - 3)(x^2 + 3x + 9)}
\end{align*}

The factorization of $x^3 - 9$ involves recognizing it can also be expressed as a difference of cubes, where $9 = 3^3$. This gives:
$$x^3 - 9 = x^3 - 3^3 = (x - 3)(x^2 + 3x + 9)$$

$x^3 + 9$ does not factor further over the real numbers as it cannot be expressed as a difference or sum of cubes with real coefficients.

